It looks like printing the conditions is inverted between prin1 and princ.


CL-USER> (handler-case (time (fact 200)) (bocl:OUT-OF-STACK-MEMORY-ERROR (c) (prin1 c) (terpri)))
Control stack exhausted (recursion too deep): used 8796924 of 8773632 bytes

NIL

CL-USER> (handler-case (time (fact 200)) (bocl:OUT-OF-STACK-MEMORY-ERROR (c) (princ c) (terpri)))
#<OUT-OF-STACK-MEMORY-ERROR 0x509cf5c00>

NIL

For a normal condition, princ shows the message:

CL-USER> (loop with e = (make-condition 'simple-error :format-control "This is a simple error ~A" :format-arguments '(42))
               for p in '(prin1 princ)
               do (princ p) (princ " --> ") (funcall p e) (terpri))
PRIN1 --> #<SIMPLE-ERROR This is a simple error 42>
PRINC --> This is a simple error 42

NIL

But for bocl:OUT-OF-STACK-MEMORY-ERROR, it's inverted:

CL-USER> (loop with e = (make-condition 'bocl:OUT-OF-STACK-MEMORY-ERROR :format-control "This out-of-stack memory error ~A" :format-arguments '(42))
               for p in '(prin1 princ)
               do (princ p) (princ " --> ") (funcall p e) (terpri))
PRIN1 --> Control stack exhausted (recursion too deep)
PRINC --> #<OUT-OF-STACK-MEMORY-ERROR 0x5097f7e48>

RESOLUTION (2026-07-18) — v1.8.56
---------------------------------
Fixed together with clos-condition-report-not-printed.issue (see its
RESOLUTION for the four changes).  The inversion came from DEFINE-
CONDITION's :REPORT print-object method reporting unconditionally
(so PRIN1 showed the message) while PRINC never reached PRINT-OBJECT
for CLOS instances (so it showed #<...>).  Now: princ/~A = the report,
prin1/~S = #<TYPE ...>, for kernel AND CLOS conditions alike.
